mcu: group the per-instruction fields ahead of the memory arrays

mcu_t puts 664 KiB of ROM, RAM, SRAM, NVRAM and card RAM arrays between the
register file and everything else the interpreter touches every instruction:
dev_register, the four peripheral pointers, the romset flags, rom2_mask,
analog_end_time and the operand-decode scratch all sit past a 656 KiB hole.
Field order is layout only -- mcu_t is never serialised, memcpy'd or aggregate
initialised anywhere in the tree -- so move the arrays to the end.

On x86-64 this changes nothing (7,556,638,426 -> 7,557,195,495 Ir, +0.007%);
addressing modes there take a 32-bit displacement.  On AArch64 a byte load with
a scaled 12-bit immediate reaches 4095 bytes from its base, so with the upstream
order GCC 13 -O3 -mcpu=cortex-a53 materialises two extra base registers on entry
to MCU_Step() and has to save and restore x21/x22 to hold them.  Counting the
instructions actually executed on the mk2 path through MCU_Step():

    upstream order   37
    this patch       32

The saving is five AArch64 instructions per emulated instruction, and two loads
additionally fold into ldp pairs.  All the fields above now live in the first
336 bytes of the struct.

